        "review": {
          "evidence": {
            "engineering-quality": {
              "availability": "available",
              "findings": [
                {
                  "evidence": "dev-platform/checks.toml (diff lines 55-58) sets patterns = [AGENTS.md, README.md, docs/**, openspec/specs/**, dev-platform/capabilities/**]. It omits explorer/nodes/**, explorer/map.toml, explorer/editorial-review.json, explorer/assets/** and scripts/build_explorer.py. A malformed node, such as an unfinished sentence, is therefore not caught by selected checks. It is caught only by the test group or the Explorer workflow.",
                  "id": "explorer-check-pattern-omits-explorer-inputs",
                  "severity": "advisory",
                  "summary": "The new `explorer` check fires only on edits to bound source files. It does not fire on edits to the Explorer's own inputs."
                },
                {
                  "evidence": "scripts/select_checks.py main() (diff lines 4593-4603) sets `code = exit_request.code`. That value may be None or a string. If `run_template` ends the process another way, such as `os.exec*` or a process-level exit, the drift step is skipped without notice. The wrapper also runs `build_explorer.py drift --base`, which builds the full site and aborts on any editorial error. A selected-checks run unrelated to the Explorer can therefore fail on an editorial or tooling problem, which contradicts the",
                  "id": "select-checks-wrapper-systemexit-handling",
                  "severity": "advisory",
                  "summary": "The `select_checks.py` wrapper catches `SystemExit` from `run_template`, and its exit-code handling is fragile. It also builds the whole site for every `--execute` run."
                },
                {
                  "evidence": "scripts/build_explorer.py mark_reviewed (diff lines 4360-4376) copies `site.digests` verbatim into the review record. The review is a single committed JSON blob with no reviewer or commit metadata. A one-line `--all` run silences all drift, so the advisory signal is easy to defeat. The note in the drift-issue body encourages running it per node, but nothing enforces that.",
                  "id": "mark-reviewed-writes-unvalidated-digest-baseline",
                  "severity": "advisory",
                  "summary": "`mark-reviewed --all` stamps whatever the sources currently contain, and it prunes obsolete records. Nothing links the stamp to an actual review."
                }
              ],
              "perspective": "engineering-quality",
              "report_sha256": "efdad8f767e11ac2badca1c0a4b1c78ef11e7633afb78d54290bbb52d389398f"
            },
            "spec-fidelity": {
              "availability": "available",
              "findings": [
                {
                  "evidence": "scripts/select_checks.py (candidate.diff ~4578-4602): with `--execute` and no `--base`, surface_explorer_drift prints an error and returns 2. main() then sets that as the exit code if the template selector succeeded. The same applies to any non-zero exit of `build_explorer.py drift --base`, for example an unresolvable merge base. Drift itself never fails, but drift reporting can fail the whole selected-checks run. This is a tool error, not drift, and the wrapper docstring says so. It is still st",
                  "id": "select-checks-drift-wrapper-can-fail",
                  "severity": "advisory",
                  "summary": "The select_checks.py wrapper can fail a selected-checks run for a reason tied to drift reporting. The delta says stale nodes must never fail any check."
                }
              ],
              "perspective": "spec-fidelity",
              "report_sha256": "533f475397872541db4bc591e04a04b21d4340cb337d148e26f3dd3dc8c3abc7"
            }
          },
